.Dd July 30, 2026
.Dt LIBNETWORKCMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm libnetworkcmp
.Nd public client transport for NetworkCmp providers
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libnetworkcmp
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In networkcmp.h
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_open "const char *component"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_client_open "const char *component" "const struct networkcmp_preferences *preferences" "struct networkcmp_client **client"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_client_fd "const struct networkcmp_client *client"
.Ft "const struct networkcmp_hello_reply *"
.Fn networkcmp_client_limits "const struct networkcmp_client *client"
.Ft void
.Fn networkcmp_client_close "struct networkcmp_client *client"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_hello "int fd" "struct networkcmp_hello_reply *reply"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_negotiate "int fd" "const struct networkcmp_preferences *preferences" "struct networkcmp_hello_reply *reply"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_attach_io "struct networkcmp_client *client" "struct networkcmp_handle socket" "uint32_t socket_type" "struct networkcmp_io **io"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn networkcmp_write "struct networkcmp_io *io" "const void *buffer" "size_t length"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn networkcmp_read "struct networkcmp_io *io" "void *buffer" "size_t capacity"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_send_datagram "struct networkcmp_io *io" "const void *buffer" "size_t length"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn networkcmp_recv_datagram "struct networkcmp_io *io" "void *buffer" "size_t capacity"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_event_fd "const struct networkcmp_io *io"
.Ft void
.Fn networkcmp_io_close "struct networkcmp_io *io"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_resolve "int fd" "const char *host" "const char *service" "uint32_t family" "uint32_t socket_type" "uint32_t flags" "struct networkcmp_resolve_result *results" "size_t *nresults" "char *canonname" "size_t canonname_size" "uint32_t *ttl_seconds"
.Ft int
.Fn networkcmp_getaddrinfo "int fd" "const char *host" "const char *service" "const struct addrinfo *hints" "struct addrinfo **result"
.Ft void
.Fn networkcmp_freeaddrinfo "struct addrinfo *result"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library defines the provider-neutral
.Dv org.5bsd.cmp.network
version
.Dv 1.0.0
interface.
Applications communicate with private or shared NetworkCmp instances through
brokered
.Xr libservice 3
channels and transfer bulk TCP or datagram data using
.Xr libshmring 3 .
No provider-specific networking structure is part of the public ABI.
.Pp
The
.Fn networkcmp_open
function opens the channel injected under the consumer manifest's local
.Fa component
name.
Passing
.Dv NULL
uses the local component name in
.Ev NETWORKCMP ,
which
.Xr serviced 8
sets when exactly one NetworkCmp session is injected or when the conventional
.Ql network
key disambiguates several sessions.
If the variable is absent, the conventional name is used.
The variable contains only a key into the sealed bootstrap descriptor and
conveys no authority.
The library never names, discovers, or falls back to a concrete provider;
.Xr serviced 8
resolves the requested interface and version before execution.
This permits alternative providers to implement the same versioned protocol
without changing application code.
.Pp
Control operations use bounded messages with opaque, generation-tagged
handles.
.Fn networkcmp_hello
performs feature negotiation.
.Fn networkcmp_client_open
is the preferred entry point.
It opens the injected channel, performs the initial negotiation, and records
the selected values.
The fields of
.Vt struct networkcmp_preferences
request TX ring size, RX ring size, and maximum datagram size.
Each ring size must be a power of two within the protocol limits.
A zero field selects the default configured for the selected serviced session
or provider.
Preferences are requests rather than authority: the reply returned by
.Fn networkcmp_client_limits
is the binding contract and may contain smaller values.
Negotiation occurs once at initial contact, not for each socket or packet.
.Fn networkcmp_negotiate
provides the same operation for callers managing a raw channel.
Typed functions are provided for socket creation, bind, connect, listen,
accept, options, shutdown, close, and name resolution.
.Fn networkcmp_resolve
implements bounded
.Xr getaddrinfo 3 Ns -style
forward resolution without exposing provider or resolver descriptors.
The caller supplies the result-array capacity through
.Fa nresults ;
on success it contains the number returned.
An optional canonical-name buffer and TTL output may be supplied.
A TTL of zero means the selected provider does not expose TTL information.
.Fn networkcmp_getaddrinfo
is the familiar convenience interface.
It translates the bounded results into a linked
.Vt struct addrinfo
list and returns an
.Dv EAI_*
status.
The resulting list must be released with
.Fn networkcmp_freeaddrinfo ,
not
.Xr freeaddrinfo 3 .
Protocol and resolver failures return
.Li -1
and set
.Va errno .
.Pp
Typed operations are synchronous.
The library serializes complete request/reply exchanges so threads cannot
consume one another's replies.
Fork handlers quiesce an in-flight exchange and leave the client lock usable
in both parent and child.
The descriptor returned by
.Fn networkcmp_open
is close-on-exec and owned by the caller.
.Pp
Bulk stream and datagram payloads use role-separated
.Xr libshmring 3
rings, while the control channel supplies wakeups and backpressure notices.
.Fn networkcmp_attach_io
creates and attaches one application-producer TX ring and one
application-consumer RX ring using the negotiated values.
.Fn networkcmp_write
and
.Fn networkcmp_read
operate on stream rings.
.Fn networkcmp_send_datagram
and
.Fn networkcmp_recv_datagram
preserve one datagram per record.
These operations are nonblocking and report ring backpressure with
.Er EAGAIN .
The library serializes producers and consumers independently, preserving the
underlying single-producer/single-consumer contract while allowing ordinary
multithreaded application use.
.Fn networkcmp_event_fd
returns the borrowed control descriptor used for
.Xr kqueue 2
notifications.
The client must outlive every IO object attached through it.
.Pp
NetworkCmp transports bytes and datagrams only.
It does not open or save files and receives no filesystem capability.
An application that wants persistence reads from NetworkCmp and writes through
its independently injected FileSystemCmp.
The injected channel is non-transferable and locked against propagation
beyond the supervised exec.
.Pp
The
.Ql networkcmp
DTrace provider exposes component-open, message-send, message-receive, and
message-reject probes.
Provider daemons audit policy decisions through OpenBSM; client-library
activity is operational telemetry and is not audited.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getaddrinfo 3 ,
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr libshmring 3
